package org.mattshoe.mtg.core

internal val storyInnistrad = StorySection(
    "innistrad", "Innistrad",
    "Innistrad's people survived the night because a vampire built them an angel, and everything since has been the cost of losing her.",
    listOf(
        StoryArticle(
            "helvault", "Helvault",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "sorins-angel", "Sorin's Angel",
                    listOf(
                        "Innistrad's vampires began in a famine. Thousands of years ago, in the land that would become Stensia, an aging alchemist named Edgar Markov went looking for a way to outlast hunger and age, and a demon named Shilgengar showed him one. The ritual needed the blood of an angel. Edgar and his sons trapped the angel Marycz in his laboratory, drained her, and made a draught of what they took. Those who drank it stopped aging and started needing blood. Edgar's family held his teenage grandson down and forced it into him, and the horror of it lit a planeswalker's spark in the boy. That boy was [Sorin](who/sorin) Markov.",
                        "Sorin left Innistrad and kept coming back. Across the centuries he watched his grandfather's children spread into human lands and hunt more boldly with every generation. He saw the arithmetic his family would not. If the vampires bled humanity dry, they would turn on one another, and the plane would empty out. Edgar told him they had eternity to find an answer. Sorin did not wait for it.",
                        "About a thousand years before this story, borrowing old human beliefs about the moon and the afterlife, Sorin made an archangel. He named her Avacyn and gave her one task, to protect Innistrad's humans while he was away. Through her, faith became real power. A church grew around her name, with cathars to fight and priests whose blessings actually worked. Some vampires understood what he had done. Most never forgave it. To them Sorin was a traitor to his own blood.",
                        "Avacyn was half of what he built. The other half was a block of silver cut from Innistrad's own moon and shaped with the stone magic Sorin had learned from the lithomancer [Nahiri](who/nahiri), his old ally from the [binding of the Eldrazi](the-binding). It stood at the edge of the courtyard of Thraben Cathedral, rough and angular, veined with dark mortar, and after Avacyn herself it became the holiest object on the plane. People called it the {Helvault}. It was built to hold the things that could not be killed.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "griselbrand", "Griselbrand",
                    listOf(
                        "With Avacyn in the sky, the balance tipped toward the living. Her host cut down vampires, broke the werewolf howlpacks along the edges of the wild, banished malevolent geists and burned ghouls with light. Humanity spread out again. But the retreat of the old monsters left room, and demons came to fill it, with swarms of devils at their heels.",
                        "Innistrad's demons are made of black mana, and killing one only sends it away for a while. Avacyn fought them one at a time and won, and they came back. So she bound them instead. She forged a silver collar for demonkind, and the collar became her holy symbol, carried by every priest and cathar of her church. With the right rites, she and her angels could draw a wicked creature into the Helvault and seal it there. Over the years they pulled in so many demons that only a handful were left loose.",
                        "The strongest of that handful was {Griselbrand}. He had never been caught, and he chose to stop hiding. Under a full moon he landed on the Helvault itself and challenged Avacyn to single combat. They fought for days, seen by no one but her angels, the Lunarch Mikaeus and his most trusted bishops. At the end of her strength Avacyn drove the demon into the vault. As he went, Griselbrand threw his spear through her heart. The binding spell recoiled, and the archangel was pulled into the silver after him.",
                        "The men who saw it swore themselves to secrecy. Mikaeus, the highest human in the church, went back to his people and told them all was well. Faith still powered the blessings, he reasoned, so faith had to be kept alive, whatever the truth. He met every question about the angel with a calm decree that said nothing. But the blessings weakened, the wards on the roadside shrines faded, and rumors spread that Avacyn had abandoned the world. Out past the village walls, everything she had driven back began to come home.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("Some priests speak in whispers of the Helvault, a silver prison of unpurged evils.", "Purify the Grave"),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "siege-of-thraben", "Siege of Thraben",
                    listOf(
                        "By the time anyone in the church admitted something was wrong, the night had the upper hand. Vampires hunted openly, werewolves came in out of the woods, and whole townships pledged themselves to any power that promised to save them. Humanity stood closer to extinction than it had in a thousand years. It was the moment the stitcher Geralf and his sister Gisa had been waiting for.",
                        "The Cecani siblings were necromancers who could barely stand each other. Geralf built skaabs, corpses stitched together and set walking with alchemy. Gisa raised ghouls. They wanted Thraben, for its standing and for the bodies beneath its streets, and for once they agreed to want it together. While their dead dug under the walls, the city's defenders held the top of them. Lothar, the Guardian of Thraben, was killed, and command of the defense passed to a young cathar named Thalia.",
                        "Geralf did not stay at the walls. He slipped behind the defense to the cathedral, knocked at the Lunarch's door, stabbed Mikaeus with a letter opener and cut out his heart as a present for his sister. Gisa, left holding the line with ghouls while her brother was away, was not grateful. The siege broke and the city held. Thalia went to report her victory and found the Lunarch's body instead. Bishop Alwin, afraid of what the news would do to a faith already cracking, had Mikaeus laid in the tomb of the Lunarchs without telling anyone.",
                        "Someone else was in Thraben that night. [Liliana](who/liliana) Vess, a necromancer and planeswalker, had come to Innistrad to kill Griselbrand. Years before, she had sold her soul to four demons for youth and power, and he was one of them. She had been hunting him across the plane with the cursed Chain Veil on her arm, while Garruk Wildspeaker, a hunter she had cursed with that same veil, hunted her. From Volpaig, leader of the demon-worshipping Skirsdag cult, she learned before she killed him that Mikaeus knew where Griselbrand had gone.",
                        "So she broke into the crypt and raised the Lunarch. The dead man got up as {Mikaeus, the Unhallowed}, the highest servant of the church turned against everything he had defended, and he told her what he had sworn never to tell. Griselbrand was inside the Helvault. So was Avacyn.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "sorin-comes-home", "Sorin Comes Home",
                    listOf(
                        "Sorin came back to find his angel gone. He had arranged Innistrad to run without him, a balance with safeguards meant to stop either side from tipping too far, and he returned to a world tearing down the very institutions he had helped found. He searched for Avacyn with grim purpose, cutting down anyone who stood between him and an answer, and found the capital of Gavony ransacked and nothing else. He is {Sorin, Lord of Innistrad} on the card, and he had rarely looked less like the lord of anything.",
                        "The search stalled when he ran into Tibalt, a young planeswalker who set himself against every authority holding the world together. Sorin judged him too dangerous to leave loose and stayed to deal with him. By the time that was done, the problem of the missing angel had solved itself, and Sorin turned to a greater threat, the Eldrazi, newly free on Zendikar.",
                        "There was something else in the Helvault, and Sorin had put it there. Long before, the Eldrazi had stirred in their prison and Nahiri had called for her two old allies. The call never reached Sorin, most likely swallowed by the Helvault itself. When Nahiri came to Innistrad to confront him, he showed no remorse. He reminded her who had taught her to be a planeswalker and told her to take it up with [Ugin](who/ugin) instead. She threatened him, Avacyn rose to defend her maker, and Sorin ended the fight by sealing Nahiri inside the Helvault. That prisoner was added to the story in 2016, years after the Helvault was first written, and she is now part of the official history.",
                        "So the silver monolith in Thraben's courtyard held the most powerful demon on Innistrad, the archangel who was the keystone of its faith, and a lithomancer nursing a grudge measured in centuries. Guarding it was Thalia, now Guardian of Thraben, who knew it as the holiest thing on the plane and had never been told what was inside. Walking toward it was Liliana Vess, who had.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Innistrad"),
                StorySource("Set", "Dark Ascension"),
                StorySource("Set", "Duel Decks Sorin vs. Tibalt"),
                StorySource("Story", "Liliana's Mission, by Doug Beyer, Savor the Flavor on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "Mikaeus, the Unhallowed, by Jenna Helland, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "Sorin's Homecoming, The Prison of Silver and The State of the Faith, by Doug Beyer, Savor the Flavor on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "Stone and Blood, by Kelly Digges, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Helvault"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Avacyn"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Mikaeus Cecani"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Sorin Markov"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "avacyn-restored", "Avacyn Restored",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "thalias-choice", "Thalia's Choice",
                    listOf(
                        "[Liliana](who/liliana) could not break the [Helvault](helvault) herself. Even Nahiri, who could bend stone like clay, had never been able to touch it. But Liliana knew how to make other people do what she could not. She wove a spell around the vault and around the cathars who guarded it, and then she gave the Guardian of Thraben a choice.",
                        "Either Thalia would destroy the Helvault, or the spell would kill every one of her soldiers. Thalia had come up through the cathar academy at the Elgaud Grounds and been raised to the elite of Thraben, sworn to the holiest object in the church. She was also a commander who would not trade her people for a stone, and Liliana had counted on exactly that. Thalia chose her soldiers.",
                        "A golden helix streaked up into the sky from the courtyard, and the silver monolith blew apart. Out of the wreckage came Avacyn, free at last, the moment drawn on {Avacyn, Angel of Hope}. Out with her came everything else the vault had held. Demons bound for centuries poured into the night, and among them was Griselbrand, the one Liliana had come for. In the same confusion, unnoticed by anyone in Thraben, [Nahiri](who/nahiri) walked free as well.",
                        "Across the plane the change was immediate. The blessings of parish priests came back. The wards carved into roadside shrines flared to life. Archmages whose holy magic had been draining away for months found it returned twice as strong, and flights of angels came down again on the beasts loose in the provinces. Fearful mobs became battalions almost overnight. Innistrad had its angel back, and her first task was the one she had been interrupted in, because the demons were out.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("A golden helix streaked skyward from the Helvault. A thunderous explosion shattered the silver monolith and Avacyn emerged, free from her prison at last.", "Avacyn, Angel of Hope"),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "griselbrands-end", "Griselbrand's End",
                    listOf(
                        "Griselbrand did not stay to face Avacyn. The moment he was free he fled, and Liliana went after him. She tracked him across Innistrad, cutting through angels and demons alike, until she had him cornered. The demon tried what demons always try. He offered her power in exchange for his life. She saw through it.",
                        "Liliana had come to Innistrad carrying the Chain Veil, an ancient artifact she had taken from a ruined temple on Shandalar, a thing of terrible strength that never stopped whispering to her. She drew on it now and destroyed Griselbrand. Innistrad's demons usually reform after death, sometimes over a thousand years or more, and the story has left open whether the Veil made his death final. Either way, the second of her four demons was gone. The card {Liliana of the Veil} is her in these years.",
                        "It cost her. Every use of the Veil hurt more than the last, and when she left Innistrad its voices went with her. But one more demon who owned a share of her soul was dead, and she had used a church, its holiest relic and the angel at its heart to kill him.",
                        "Avacyn turned on the rest. She rallied her host and set out to contain the released demons and cleanse the plane of them, and the remains of her old prison went to war with her. The silver of the broken Helvault was gathered up and given to the finest lunar smiths in the church, and a family of holy swords was forged from it. Even the rubble was put to use, beaten into {Bladed Bracers} for the faithful. Wherever Avacyn's flights passed, the things that had grown bold in her absence learned to fear the sky again.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "the-cursemute", "The Cursemute",
                    listOf(
                        "Of all the curses on Innistrad, lycanthropy was the one that broke families. A werewolf was a neighbor by day and a killer under the moon. In the remote village of Gatstaf, after Avacyn's return, the people begged her to lift the curse, and they gathered in a grotto where many had come to strike down the werewolves among them.",
                        "Avacyn came. Her answer was short. She could not break the curse, because it bound the human spirit to the spirit of the wild, and destroying one would destroy the other. What she could do was change it. If the werewolves of Gatstaf would swear to guard humanity, she would fuse the two halves of them and let them live in the light of her blessing. They accepted.",
                        "The spell that followed rolled out from the grotto across the whole plane. Werewolves became wolfir, great wolf creatures sworn to the church, the kind of guardian drawn on {Wolfir Silverheart}. Some of the afflicted were cured outright and went home as ordinary people. Other foul curses broke in the same wave. It was called the cursemute, and for a village pariah it meant something as plain as walking down the street without being shamed. The card {Cursebreak} tells it in one line about a woman named Sigrun, finally free.",
                        "No one at Gatstaf could know that the cursemute would last only as long as the angel who made it stayed herself. For now it held. The church that had nearly collapsed was full again. The howlpacks scattered, hunted down by angels, and the gryffs came back to the skies. Innistrad had not been so safe in living memory.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "a-year-of-peace", "A Year of Peace",
                    listOf(
                        "What followed was the best year anyone could remember. For roughly a year after the Helvault broke, Avacyn led Innistrad's humans through a peace and prosperity the plane had rarely known. Her angels answered prayers. Her church ran the provinces. The monsters kept to the dark, and for once people could believe they would stay there.",
                        "It drew the curious. Tamiyo, a moonfolk scholar and planeswalker from Kamigawa, had spent many cycles studying Innistrad's great silver moon and how it drove the plane's werewolves and vampires, and she had worked for months in the moonstone tower of the Nephalian astronomancer Jenrik. When the Helvault opened, she began filling her scrolls with new questions, about the angel, the wolfir, the returning gryffs, the newly empowered holy wards and the nature of the vault itself.",
                        "[Sorin](who/sorin) had already gone. His angel was back where he had put her, and his attention had turned to the Eldrazi, who had [broken out of their prison on Zendikar](rise). It was the prison he had helped build, with Nahiri and with [Ugin](who/ugin), and the three had sworn to return if it ever failed.",
                        "Nahiri had kept that oath and been left alone with it. Out of the shattered vault she went home to Zendikar and found it in ruins. The Eldrazi were loose, continents lay wasted, and her allies seemed to have forsaken her. She swore that Innistrad would bleed the way Zendikar had bled. Then she came back. The peace lasted until the end of the next Harvest Moon, and what ended it is told in [Shadows](shadows).",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Avacyn Restored"),
                StorySource("Story", "The Guardian, the Witch, and the Angel, by Doug Beyer, Savor the Flavor on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "Angel's Rise and Demon's Release, by Doug Beyer, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "The Lunarch's Journal, by Colin Kawakami, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Avacyn Restored"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Thalia"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Griselbrand"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Innistrad (plane)"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Tamiyo"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "shadows", "Shadows",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "harvest-moon", "Harvest Moon",
                    listOf(
                        "The madness began with a mother and a lost child. Near the end of the Harvest Moon, a year after [Avacyn's return](avacyn-restored), a village outcast named Kelse prayed to the archangel to find her son Maeli, who had wandered into the woods. Avacyn could hear every prayer on Innistrad. She came, as she always had, and she found the boy.",
                        "As she did, a strange spell struck her. It showed her a vision of the stonework of [Nahiri](who/nahiri) and filled her head with thoughts that were not her own, thoughts that humanity was corrupt. In the same moment Kelse, seized by a rage that was not entirely hers either, turned on the son she had just been given back. Avacyn watched a mother punish a rescued child, and for the first time she felt disgust for the people she was made to protect.",
                        "It grew. The source, as was later revealed, was a web of standing stones called cryptoliths, raised across Innistrad by Nahiri to drag the plane's leylines toward a single point. Angels are made of raw mana, and the redirected flow poisoned them first. When the Lunarch Macher, unsure of his goddess, prayed for proof of her power, she answered by humiliating him until he fainted. Black began to creep into her eyes.",
                        "As her mind turned, the cursemute failed. Wolfir who had been made whole fell back into werewolves, and many of them killed their own families before they understood what was happening. One, a man named Liont, prayed to Avacyn in horror at what he had done. She broke through his roof and killed him.",
                        "By the Hunter's Moon she came to the archangel Sigarda with Bruna and Gisela at her sides, both already lost to the same madness. Avacyn now spoke openly of exterminating humanity. Sigarda would not answer, and Avacyn blew the roof off her home as a warning. Then Avacyn returned to Kelse's village with a flight of angels and slaughtered it. Kelse told Maeli to run, and Avacyn burned her for it. The card {Descend upon the Sinful} shows what such a night looked like. The guardian on {Archangel Avacyn} had become the one on its other face.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("Wings that once bore hope are now stained with blood. She is our guardian no longer.", "Grete, cathar apostate, on Avacyn, the Purifier"),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "markov-manor", "Markov Manor",
                    listOf(
                        "[Jace](who/jace) Beleren was looking for [Sorin](who/sorin). The mind mage had just helped destroy two of the three Eldrazi titans in the [Battle for Zendikar](battle-for-zendikar), and the spirit dragon [Ugin](who/ugin) had told the new [Gatewatch](gatewatch) that Sorin, one of the titans' original jailers, would be far less forgiving about it than he was. So Jace came to Innistrad to find the vampire and ask him to stand with them.",
                        "His first stop was the estate of [Liliana](who/liliana) Vess. She had come home to rest, and she did not want him going anywhere near Sorin. His second stop was Markov Manor, Sorin's ancestral home, and there he found only ruin. The house had been twisted into impossible shapes, as if its stone had been kneaded, and the vampires of House Markov were set into the masonry, alive and in agony. It was a message written in stone, the moment on {Declaration in Stone}, and it was meant for Sorin.",
                        "In the wreckage Jace found a journal. It belonged to Tamiyo, and it followed the cryptoliths. Jace followed them too, across a countryside where people were going strange, and met a woman raving about a temple at the Drownyard, a reef off the coast of Nephalia where many ships had been lost. When he tried to read her mind, he found it crowded with illusions of himself. At the Drownyard Temple he found the dead tending the stones and angels gathering overhead. Tamiyo's notes suggested the stones were pulling ley energy to that one place to draw some vast celestial body toward Innistrad, like a second moon. That is {Epiphany at the Drownyard}.",
                        "Then a buzzing voice told him Liliana was behind it all. Half mad himself by now, Jace went back to her manor and struck at her mind. Liliana could have killed him, and was urged to. She refused, and the refusal brought him back to himself long enough to talk. When the madness rose in him again, he asked her the way to Thraben. He meant to confront Avacyn.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("Is this what you found, Tamiyo?", "Jace Beleren, Epiphany at the Drownyard"),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "lunarch-inquisition", "Lunarch Inquisition",
                    listOf(
                        "While Jace chased stones, the church was eating itself. Mikaeus's successor as Lunarch had died early in Avacyn's madness, and in his place the senior bishops formed a Lunarch Council, with a few cathar commanders given seats to advise but no vote. One of them was Odric, a soldier with no patience for church politics who had done more than anyone to bring the council together. He is {Odric, Lunarch Marshal}.",
                        "The council met the crisis with an inquisition more terrifying than any the church had run before. Its hunters went village to village looking for sin and found it everywhere. Werewolves who had been wolfir a season ago were hunted down. Thalia, the cathar who had broken the Helvault to save her soldiers, watched the church she served grow into something its own people feared.",
                        "Then she found the rot at its center. The Skirsdag, the demon-worshipping cult whose leader Liliana had killed during the siege of Thraben, had taken control of the council itself, in the service of the demon Ormendahl. Odric had been made to betray his own people, and it broke him. Thalia could not reform a church whose leaders served a demon, so she left it. With Odric and his lieutenant Grete, she founded the Order of Saint Traft. The card {Thalia, Heretic Cathar} is her after that break.",
                        "Saint Traft had been a young priest of Stensia and a famous slayer of demons, until the demons tricked him into killing innocents and the demon lord Withengar murdered him. He never passed into the Blessed Sleep. He became a geist instead, and it was said that wherever his apparition appeared, the angel who failed to protect him in life was never far behind. He is {Geist of Saint Traft}. Thalia's order would need every saint it could find. Avacyn's angels were hunting the people the church had sworn to protect, the church itself was in a demon's hands, and between Innistrad's humans and the night stood a cathar who had walked out of her own faith.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "olivias-price", "Olivia's Price",
                    listOf(
                        "Sorin came home to the same ruin Jace had found. Markov Manor was twisted out of shape, his family set screaming into its walls, and the message was clear enough. His past had come back for him. To fight Nahiri he would need an army, and the vampires of Innistrad had shunned him for a thousand years, ever since he built the angel who hunted them. He could not call the bloodlines himself.",
                        "So he went to the remote estate of Olivia Voldaren. She had drunk from Marycz in Edgar Markov's first ritual, which made her one of the oldest and most powerful vampires on the plane, and she had the standing to summon the bloodlines to war. She is {Olivia, Mobilized for War}. Sorin asked for her help. She named her price, and it was simple. Sorin would destroy Avacyn.",
                        "He agreed. The angel he had made to protect humanity was now killing it by the village, with Bruna and Gisela and their flights beside her, and the church that bore her name could do nothing to stop her. Whatever Sorin felt about it, he set out for Thraben to end what he had begun a thousand years before.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "thraben-cathedral", "Thraben Cathedral",
                    listOf(
                        "In Thraben, Jace found Tamiyo. She cured him of the stones' madness, and he persuaded her to face Avacyn with him. Tamiyo did not want the angel destroyed, because Avacyn's power still warded the whole plane against evils no one had yet named. It did not matter. Avacyn came for them in Thraben Cathedral, and the two planeswalkers were losing when Sorin arrived.",
                        "Avacyn did not know him. Her spells would not touch him, and when he told her he was her maker, the madness drew back for a moment. In that moment she understood what she had done, and she turned it on him. He had made her, so he had made the flaw in her, and every innocent she had killed was on him. She called the angels of Innistrad down on him. He beat them. Then the madness returned, and with it the power to hurt him.",
                        "They fought until the cathedral nearly came down. Sorin drained her and drove her through the chapel floor into the vault beneath, the place where he had first made her. There he offered to heal her and make her anew, her mind cleansed. She refused, and swore she would fight him forever rather than be altered by him. She expected to be bound, the way she had bound the demons. She could be destroyed. Sorin unravelled the spell that had made her, a thousand years after he first cast it, and turned his face away as Avacyn came apart into ash. The card is {Anguished Unmaking}.",
                        "With her went the last of the protections Sorin had woven over Innistrad. Jace started asking him why, and as he spoke, Sorin saw the shape of it. The cryptoliths, the maddened angels, the plane stripped of every safeguard it had. He had done Nahiri's work for her. Then Emrakul arrived on Innistrad. What she was and how she was brought there is told in [Emrakul on Innistrad](emrakul-on-innistrad). What she did to the plane is told in [Eldritch Moon](eldritch-moon).",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("Sorin had created Avacyn, so it was a cruelty beyond imagining, a pain beyond description, that it fell upon him to end her forever.", "Anguished Unmaking"),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Shadows over Innistrad"),
                StorySource("Story", "A Gaze Blank and Pitiless, by Ken Troop, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "Unwelcome, The Mystery of Markov Manor and Drownyard Temple, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "Promises Old and New, by Ari Levitch, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "Lunarch Inquisition, by James Wyatt, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "Stories and Endings, by Nik Davidson, and I Am Avacyn, by Doug Beyer, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Shadows over Innistrad"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Avacyn"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Odric"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Traft"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Olivia Voldaren"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "eldritch-moon", "Eldritch Moon",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "hanweir", "Hanweir",
                    listOf(
                        "Emrakul did not conquer Innistrad. She changed it. The Eldrazi titan, called to the plane by [Nahiri](who/nahiri) as a weapon against [Sorin](who/sorin), a story told in [Emrakul on Innistrad](emrakul-on-innistrad), warped living things simply by being near them. Plants, animals and people sprouted tentacles, extra eyes and extra limbs in her strange lattice patterns and colors, by degrees the survivors learned to name, distorted, disfigured, deformed, misshapen and monstrous. Some of the changed fused together into new creatures. She is {Emrakul, the Promised End}.",
                        "The village of Hanweir in Gavony left the clearest record, because it had a newspaper. During the angels' madness, told in [Shadows](shadows), Hanweir declared itself independent of its parish and its province, built new walls and rang its bell until a new bell had to be cast. Then Emrakul's influence set in. The town's women began giving birth only to twins. The Hanweir Chronicle stopped printing the names of its writers. The council made meals at the church mandatory and ruled that no one who came into the town could ever leave.",
                        "In the end the whole village and everyone in it became one creature. On the cards, the town's battlements and its garrison meld into {Hanweir, the Writhing Township}. One of the last entries in the Chronicle suggests that Hanweir, as it was by then, had trouble holding a pen. It has not been seen since, and its fate is unknown.",
                        "Hanweir was one town. All across the plane the same thing was happening by degrees, and the angels, who should have been Innistrad's first defense, were the first to fall.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "the-fallen-sisters", "The Fallen Sisters",
                    listOf(
                        "Bruna and Gisela were two of four archangel sisters older than Avacyn. Bruna, the Light of Alabaster, warded humanity against harm in death. Gisela fought monsters with fire and fervor. Both had followed Avacyn into madness, and Emrakul took them further. {Bruna, the Fading Light} came to see only Emrakul's visions, and {Gisela, the Broken Blade} to hear only her murmurs. Then the titan conscripted what was left of them and melded the two sisters into a single creature, {Brisela, Voice of Nightmares}, her herald, still shaped something like an angel and nothing like one.",
                        "There had been four sisters. The fourth, Liesa, had made a pact with a demon a thousand years before, and Avacyn had destroyed her and her entire flight for it and forbidden her name to be spoken. The others had agreed with the judgment and stood aside. Now, of the three who were left, two belonged to Emrakul.",
                        "The third was Sigarda, the Host of Herons, who warded the living. She had never felt close to Avacyn, and she had wept and vowed to fight when the mad archangel came to her door. When she discovered what her sisters had become, she could only weep again. She took her Flight of Herons against the corrupted angels, and people across Innistrad began to pray to her, because she alone had not turned her host on them. She is {Sigarda, Host of Herons}.",
                        "Brisela was flying toward Thraben, where the last of Innistrad's defenders were gathering, and Sigarda was going after her.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("Upon discovering what had become of her sisters, Sigarda could only weep.", "Brisela, Voice of Nightmares"),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "sorin-in-stone", "Sorin in Stone",
                    listOf(
                        "Sorin had decided Innistrad could not be saved. What he could give it was vengeance. Olivia Voldaren called the bloodlines, and the vampire armies marched on the ruins of Markov Manor, where Nahiri waited with her cultists and her Eldrazi abominations. The card {Campaign of Vengeance} is that march.",
                        "Olivia led her host against the cultists. When Nahiri finally came for Sorin, Olivia stepped between them. She told Nahiri that Sorin had earned all of it and that she would love to let it happen, but he had asked for her help, and she would not allow it. Nahiri loosed a swarm of Eldrazi on Olivia's vampires to keep them busy and turned on Sorin alone. It was centuries of grievance in one fight, and the card {Nahiri's Wrath} keeps what she shouted at him.",
                        "Nahiri was badly wounded, but she won. She sealed Sorin inside the stone of his own house and planeswalked away, leaving him to watch Emrakul tear his world apart. Then Olivia came to stand over him. She proclaimed herself the new lord of Innistrad, took his sword and left him there. Then she turned her army toward Thraben, where the living were making their last stand.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("There is no saving this world, not anymore. But we shall be its bloody vengeance.", "Sorin Markov, Campaign of Vengeance"),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "battle-of-thraben", "Battle of Thraben",
                    listOf(
                        "Thraben was where Emrakul's horrors were thickest, and it was where everyone who was left came to fight. Thalia's Order of Saint Traft rode in. Olivia's vampires came from the ruins of Markov Manor, and the cathars and the vampires, who had spent a thousand years at each other's throats, agreed to a truce to purge the city together. It did not make them friends. Inside the walls Olivia killed Seeta, leader of the Sinpurged, in front of Thalia.",
                        "Thalia carried Avacyn's own spear, a gift from Rem Karolus, and she carried something else. The geist of Saint Traft had joined with her, shielding her mind from Emrakul and fighting through her. When Brisela came down on the city, Thalia could not match it alone. Sigarda fell on her sisters' fused body, Traft healed Thalia's wounds and wrapped her in a light that looked like the angel whose spear she held, and together the cathar and the archangel killed Brisela. Then Emrakul's presence drove Traft out of her, and Thalia came close to losing her mind.",
                        "At the heart of the city the [Gatewatch](gatewatch), fetched from Zendikar by [Jace](who/jace), were being overrun, the moment shown on {Emrakul's Influence}. Salvation came out of the graves. [Liliana](who/liliana) Vess had been ready to abandon Innistrad, and changed her mind. She raised an army of the dead on her way to Thraben, and even Gisa and Geralf, who could hardly stand each other, set their feud aside to fight the Eldrazi, the uneasy pair on {Gisa and Geralf}. The ghouls surged through the streets, the moment on {Dark Salvation}.",
                        "Facing the titan herself, Liliana drew on the Chain Veil harder than she ever had, enough to strike at Emrakul directly, and it nearly killed her. Then the Gatewatch, with Tamiyo, sealed Emrakul inside Innistrad's silver moon, the same moon whose silver had once made the Helvault. That is {Imprisoned in the Moon}. For the people in the streets of Thraben, the titan was simply gone.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("Countless ghouls surged through Thraben's streets, and with them came the city's salvation.", "Dark Salvation"),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "the-travails", "The Travails",
                    listOf(
                        "The years of upheaval that ended with Avacyn dead and Emrakul sealed came to be called the Travails. Thraben was a ruin. Liliana left the plane with the Gatewatch, now one of them, and she left her army behind, the dead still wandering the broken streets of the city they had saved. Bishops who had died in the fighting walked among them.",
                        "Of all Innistrad's angels, only Sigarda and her Flight of Herons came through uncorrupted. People turned to her because she alone had not turned on them, and in the years that followed a new sect grew inside the church. Sigardian priests preferred the parish to the cathedral, the local elder to distant authority, the old holidays to the grandeur of Thraben. The terror of the mad angels had pushed people away from far-off symbols of divinity and toward one another. The church's power waned, and humanity began to look to other faiths.",
                        "Sorin was still sealed in the stone of his house. Olivia had his sword. The great sigil the sealing spell had left on the moon's face faded from view, and inside, the titan slept.",
                        "Astronomancers and the witches of the deep woods began to wonder whether something that large could sleep inside a moon without changing what the moon did. About two years later, the nights began to grow longer. That is told in [Eternal Night](eternal-night).",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Eldritch Moon"),
                StorySource("Story", "Emrakul Rises, by Kimberly J. Kreines, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "Innistrad's Last Hope, by Doug Beyer, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "Campaign of Vengeance, by Ari Levitch, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "Saint Traft and the Flight of Nightmares, by James Wyatt, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "Battle of Thraben, by Nik Davidson, and The Promised End, by Ken Troop, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "The Hanweir Chronicle, by Alison Luhrs"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Eldritch Moon"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Hanweir"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Sigarda"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Brisela"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Innistrad (plane)"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "eternal-night", "Eternal Night",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "longer-nights", "Longer Nights",
                    listOf(
                        "About two years after the [Travails](eldritch-moon/the-travails), the sun began to fail. Each day it rose a little later and set a little sooner. A strange frost crept over the fields, the crops dwindled, and the days turned cold. With Emrakul asleep inside the moon, the old balance of day and night had broken, and on Innistrad a longer night meant more of everything that hunted in it. People began to fear the Eternal Night, a night that would never end.",
                        "The werewolves felt it first. Tovolar, alpha of the Mondronen howlpack, had been driven into hiding when Avacyn returned. Now he gathered a new pack of dire werewolves and set out to unite every howlpack under his rule, preaching the freedom to embrace the beast within. Many werewolves walked out of their human homes to join him. Once he had the numbers, he meant to stop anyone who tried to bring the sun back. He is {Tovolar, Dire Overlord}.",
                        "Without Avacyn, humanity turned to older magic. In the Ulvenwald, Katilda, a healer from the ruined town of Avabruck, led the Dawnhart Coven, hedge witches who had revived the worship of an ancient forest deity. She had a plan. Hidden in the woods stood the Celestus, a vast broken orrery built by witches centuries ago to track the stars and the moon. With the strength of a great gathering of people behind it, Katilda believed it could set day and night right. So she revived Harvesttide, a festival older than the church, to bring the people there.",
                        "She needed one more thing, the Moonsilver Key that opened the gold lock at the heart of the Celestus, taken from the coven centuries before. To find it she turned to Arlinn Kord, a werewolf planeswalker who had run with Tovolar's pack as a girl and left it after a fight that scarred them both. Tovolar invited Arlinn to join his great hunt. She refused and went for him, and her own wolves, Redtooth, Streak, Patience and Boulder, stepped between them and stayed with him. Arlinn said goodbye to them and went back to Katilda alone. She is {Arlinn, the Pack's Hope}.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "the-moonsilver-key", "The Moonsilver Key",
                    listOf(
                        "Arlinn went to Ravnica for help and came back with three planeswalkers. [Teferi](who/teferi), the old time mage of Dominaria. Kaya, an assassin who could pass through solid things like a ghost. [Chandra](who/chandra) Nalaar, the pyromancer. With a cathar named Adeline, they started in Thraben, where the church might have kept the key.",
                        "Thraben was a city of the dead. [Liliana](who/liliana) Vess's army still wandered its ruins, and the necromancer Gisa Cecani had taken it over and made the city her own. Kaya found an old book showing that a witch had given the key, long ago, to the Betzold family. Arlinn and Adeline knew a bishop named Worrin Betzold. They found him among the zombies, still preaching in a ruined church. He seemed to know Arlinn. All he could give her was a name, Dennick. Arlinn ended him so he could rest.",
                        "In Gavony the Betzold mansion was haunted. Kaya went in alone and cleared out the ghosts so the others could follow, and the spirit of the bishop's son Dennick told them where the key had gone. His great-grandfather had given it to a vampire for safekeeping. The vampire was [Sorin](who/sorin) Markov.",
                        "Sorin was brooding in the ruins of Markov Manor, free of [Nahiri](who/nahiri)'s stone and grieving for the angel he had unmade. Every safeguard he had built for Innistrad had fallen, he told them, and if his family wanted to sink into the long night he would let them. He refused them the key out of spite. When Arlinn mentioned Avacyn, he attacked her. When she mentioned Avacyn again, he attacked again, and this time an angel stopped his sword. Arlinn had prayed before they went in, and Sigarda had answered. While the archangel fought Sorin, the others went into the manor and found the key resting on a statue of Avacyn whose head Sorin had cut off.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("As Sigarda and Sorin battled, Arlinn and Teferi recovered the key from the Markov catacombs.", "Secrets of the Key"),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "harvesttide", "Harvesttide",
                    listOf(
                        "They reached the Celestus as Harvesttide reached its height. People had come from every province, from the Kessig foothills, the moors of Gavony, the ports of Nephalia and the sunless streets of Stensia, carrying candles, baskets of flowers and fruit, and wicker effigies of werewolves and vampires to burn. As the key arrived, Tovolar attacked.",
                        "The witches' wards held his army back for a while. When they failed, Teferi bent time to delay the sunset, and the werewolves, still in human shape, could not complete their change. Witch, werewolf and cathar stood together around the Sungold Lock, the moment on {Defend the Celestus}. Kaya, untouchable, carried the key straight through the fighting to Katilda, and the ritual began.",
                        "Arlinn found Tovolar. She beat him with weapons and her bare hands until he called her old wolves to his side and she faltered, and then he had her at his mercy. He did not kill her. He wanted her in the pack. Then Teferi's spell ran out, the sun went down, and both of them changed. In wolf form they tore each other open. Arlinn's pack went over to Tovolar, all but Patience, who stood apart, the betrayal on {Pack's Betrayal}. Arlinn pretended to give in. When Tovolar lowered his guard, she caught him in a death grip, and he yielded and led his pack away.",
                        "At the center of the Celestus the witches were nearly done. Arlinn gave her blood, and Katilda gave her soul into the key. Then Olivia Voldaren swept down from the sky, caught Katilda in midair and demanded the key. The witches said only Katilda could finish the rite. Arlinn threw the key to the vampire. Olivia hurled Katilda at the ground, and Arlinn threw herself under the falling witch to break her fall. Olivia flew off with the key, and the dawn went with her. The Eternal Night had begun, and Olivia, on {Olivia's Midnight Ambush}, promised them an invitation to her wedding.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("And you were almost finished with that ritual, weren't you, darling? How very rude of me. To make it up to you, I'll send you an invitation to my wedding.", "Olivia Voldaren, Olivia's Midnight Ambush"),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "the-crimson-wedding", "The Crimson Wedding",
                    listOf(
                        "In the long dark, the vampires of Stensia began collecting blood tithes from the living. Olivia had bigger plans. After Sigarda's fight with Sorin she captured the archangel and bound her, bleeding, to the ceiling of her hall with enchanted ribbons. Then she stole the coffin of Edgar Markov, Sorin's grandfather, from the crypt where he slept through the centuries. She would marry him. The marriage would unite the greatest bloodlines under her, and Edgar's blood magic, Sigarda's blood and the key together would give her command of Innistrad's angels. She is {Olivia, Crimson Bride}.",
                        "Arlinn's company went back to Markov Manor and found Sorin in the crypt, furious that Olivia had stolen his grandfather. They made a pact. But the Voldaren estate was warded, and only invited guests could enter. Sorin could pass and the others could not, so he walked into the wedding alone, through a crowd that laughed at him. Olivia had the coffin brought in and woke Edgar with her own blood, charming him as he woke, and the confused old vampire agreed to marry her. That is {Edgar's Awakening}. Sorin tried to stop it and was dragged away in holy chains.",
                        "Elsewhere in the dark, the vampire Henrika Domnathi was turning captured cathars into vampires for the demon Ormendahl. Thalia and Odric fought her followers and lost. When a blade came for Thalia, Odric threw himself in front of it, and Henrika bit him as he lay weakened. His blood burned her spawn like holy water. Odric rose a vampire whom the moon did not harm, his blessed sword still shining. He is {Odric, Blood-Cursed}, and his strange new life gave him back the faith the inquisition had broken.",
                        "At the altar, during the vows, Sorin attacked Olivia. His blow woke something in the key. Katilda's spirit poured out of it and cut Sigarda free, and the archangel's fury shattered every stained-glass window in the hall, killing or wounding many of the guests. The wards fell, and the others stormed in. Kaya broke Olivia's hold on the geists of her train. Arlinn caught up with Olivia, was cut badly by her blade, changed, lost herself to rage and was run through from behind. Sorin chased his grandfather into the blood pits of the sanguitorium, was beaten and thrown into one, drank deep, and climbed out with a greatsword. He found Edgar attacking Teferi, beat him and let him go.",
                        "Arlinn woke to find Tovolar and her old pack beside her. He had answered her call. Nothing had changed between them, she told him, but she took his help, and together they hunted Olivia down. They found her with Edgar, who was pleading with her to give it up. Olivia dropped the key and fled with her groom through a broken window, a step ahead of Tovolar's jaws.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "sungold-lock", "Sungold Lock",
                    listOf(
                        "Teferi hurried them back to the Celestus by bending time. Katilda's spirit came with them, and on the way she and Arlinn talked about Tovolar, and Arlinn accepted that a reckoning with him would come one day. At the Celestus, Katilda was returned to her body, and the witches of the Dawnhart Coven took their places once more. She is {Katilda, Dawnhart Martyr}.",
                        "The Moonsilver Key was shaped like an offering bowl, and the Sungold Lock at the center of the Celestus was shaped the same, so that together they made a sphere. When they met, light flooded the ancient orrery, warm as sunlight. The Celestus shook off centuries of overgrowth and its great arms began to turn. With each pass the light grew stronger, until a single column ran from the Celestus to the moon. When it faded, the moon was already sinking. The balance between night and day was restored.",
                        "Katilda lent the key to Teferi, for his studies of time and his fight against the Phyrexians. Before he left, Teferi warned Arlinn to watch for mechanical creatures, an old enemy of his coming back. Sorin, who had spent the whole affair insisting he wanted nothing to do with any of them, seemed by the end to recognize something of himself in the others and their need to keep travelling. Arlinn went back to the Kessig woods and her pack, and was happy there.",
                        "Teferi's warning was not idle. The key became part of the device that would send his spirit back in time against Phyrexia, and when the [invasion](invasion-of-the-multiverse) came, it came to Innistrad too. Arlinn and Sorin had spent the time since preparing its defenses together. Thalia fought from the back of a giant frog in {Thalia and The Gitrog Monster}, and Gisa and Geralf learned that Innistrad's zombies could not be compleated, because the glistening oil works on living minds. Thousands still died. But the sun kept rising.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Innistrad: Midnight Hunt"),
                StorySource("Set", "Innistrad: Crimson Vow"),
                StorySource("Story", "Innistrad: Midnight Hunt episodes 1 to 5, by K. Arsenault Rivera, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "Innistrad: Crimson Vow episodes, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "The Blessing of Blood, by Marcus Terrell Smith, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Innistrad: Midnight Hunt"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Innistrad: Crimson Vow"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Arlinn Kord"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Tovolar"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Katilda"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Moonsilver Key"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Olivia Voldaren"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Edgar Markov"),
            ),
        ),
    ),
)
